Z||000033||Dan86gt3.8||03-14-2000||01:29 AM||||Yes I was also wondering where I could purchase the official street racing handbook, so I could go through the rules section and set up my fiero accordingly.  So sinse NOS boosts hp, and the car doesen't have the hp/torque it is "supposed to have" then I should take out my 3.8, and put the old 2.8 back in, cause the guy next to me thinks I should have the 2.8? I mean according to the rules I should have only 2.8 kind of power.  my point is hp, is hp anyway you can get it.  I think that is the funnest part of street racing, that is not knowing what the guy in the next lane has, is that a stock 5.0 or is it a 10 second street car?  who knows ehhh? when the light turns green is when you find out, or at least it's when I find out what they guy has, so if a fiesta blows my doors off, hey more power to him, hey I might just go get my own fiesta and slap a shot on it! untill then I guess we need the president to issue an order to return all cars to stock condition, according to the "official rules"?||||reg||8
Z||000034||Greg Piet||03-14-2000||03:30 PM||||As I said in an above post, there ARE rules, which are either discussed prior to the race, or the unspoken ones which are specific to a location.  For example...   when I used to race (looking back at those days I was a sick unit) it was a given to look under the hood if there was anything on the line (be it money ot titles).  There was also the UNSPOKEN rule that if you were running NOS, you had an obligation to show your blue bottle to your competitor.  Failure to disclose the blue bottle would result in the race results being stricken from the unofficial (word of mouth) record, the wager would be not be paid off and the NOS user who tried to hide it would be marked as an untrustworthy competitor in the local street racing scene.  We also had the rule that there was not be ramming, bumping, rubbing or any other contact while racing(which again was unspoken).<P>These rules did not apply for the stoplight rally(except the hitting one) since that is normally for giggles, and is done more of on a whim than anything else.  ||||reg||1
Z||000035||GT Bastard||03-14-2000||04:10 PM||||Well well well... A fine little quarrel we have ourselves here.  I must admit that I'm a little divided on the issue of nitrous use myself, but I'm beginning to come around.  The whole idea of HP that needs to be refilled is a little cheasy, but HP is HP.  It really isn't much different than turbo's or superchargers except for the fact that nitrous isn't constant.  I'm looking into adding nitrous as a "novelty" of sorts, but I still plan to squeeze as much naturally aspirated horses as possible from my six pack. I used to think guns were "cowardly" until I bought one.  It really just depends on how it's used though.  If I use my gun to mug someone, I'm a coward.  If I use it to defend myself, it's a tool. Nitrous can be either as well, depending upon how it's used.  ||||reg||2
Z||000036||Eric||03-14-2000||04:59 PM||||This is a fight?  If so, I want in too.  I think I'm gonna be on GT Bastard's team cuz he has the guns.<P>Listen up people.  These little motors can only make so much horsepower without some form of forced induction or nitrous.  Not everyone has the cash for an all motor Fiero that runs 14s.  Don't come down on me for not trying.  I spent a whole lot of extra money making my motor into a 3.1 and it still barely cracks 16 seconds.  A better option would have been nitrous to begin with.||||reg||8
